          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the clinical case presented, what is the primary functional consequence when pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium undergoes squamous metaplasia in the larynx?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Increased mucus secretion by the newly formed squamous cells</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Stratified squamous epithelium does not secrete mucus — it is a protective lining that lacks goblet cells. The key functional loss in metaplasia is the absence of cilia needed for mucus clearance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Loss of mucus clearance because the replacement squamous cells lack cilia</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium uses cilia to sweep mucus upward. Stratified squamous epithelium is tougher but lacks cilia entirely, so mucociliary clearance is lost.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Immediate transformation of the metaplastic cells into carcinoma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Metaplasia itself is a protective adaptation, not malignancy. Only when metaplasia is accompanied by dysplasia — abnormal cell morphology with a high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio — does it signal a step toward malignancy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Thickening of the basement membrane causing airway narrowing</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Basement membrane thickening is not the primary consequence of squamous metaplasia. The critical functional loss is the absence of cilia on the replacement epithelium, impairing mucus clearance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the narration: pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium clears mucus via cilia. When replaced by stratified squamous epithelium, those cilia are lost — that is the primary functional consequence."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The key insight is that metaplasia trades specialised function (ciliary clearance) for mechanical protection — a clinically important trade-off."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
