          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which structural defect in the ciliary axoneme causes the immotile cilia and sit</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which structural defect in the ciliary axoneme causes the immotile cilia and situs inversus seen in Kartagener syndrome?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Disorganisation of the nine outer microtubule doublets</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The outer doublets are present in Kartagener syndrome. The defect lies in the dynein arms attached to those doublets — without dynein, the sliding mechanism that powers ciliary beating cannot occur.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Absence of the central microtubule pair</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The central pair is part of the normal 9+2 arrangement, but its absence is not the defect in Kartagener syndrome. The cause is absent dynein arms on the nine outer doublets.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Loss of connexon channels between adjacent ciliated cells</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Connexons form gap junctions for intercellular communication — they are not part of the ciliary axoneme. Kartagener syndrome involves a defect within the cilium itself: absent dynein arms.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Absence of dynein arms on the outer microtubule doublets</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Dynein arms generate the sliding force between outer microtubule doublets that powers ciliary beating. Without them, cilia are immotile — causing impaired mucociliary clearance and, through defective nodal cilia in embryogenesis, situs inversus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Recall the axoneme: nine outer microtubule doublets surround a central pair. Dynein arms on the outer doublets slide them to generate movement. In Kartagener syndrome, these dynein arms are absent."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. The 9+2 axoneme with dynein arms is a high-yield ultrastructural detail — it links histology to clinical genetics."
                }
              ],
